## The exact step's ending, shared

The product, the quotient and the conversion each end the same way, so the ending is written once, in
a crate-private module the three read. Each forms a numerator in the four-limb integer and a
denominator `D` from one up to `2^256 - 1` in the same four limbs. The floor of the numerator over `D`
is the position's floor, held in the same four limbs, and the residue over `D`, reduced to lowest terms,
is the remainder. A numerator that leaves the four limbs is a step not formed, and a formed step has a
position: nothing is said about the slot index, since the map reads the floor where it is. A corner is
formed when its numerator is. Each operation also starts from the phase as `n / d` in lowest
terms with `d` positive, which the module reduces once.

## Division, the quotient by a member that is non-zero by type

The quotient is admitted by the same rule as the product. Two members at a constant quantum `q` are
`(phase + a) q` and `(phase + b) q`, so their quotient is `(phase + a) / (phase + b)`, which is the
member at position `(phase + a) / ((phase + b) q) - phase`. Wherever `phase + b` is not zero that is a
function of the declared signature, at every phase and every radix, and at the zero it is not a
function at all. So the quotient is admitted over a divisor that cannot be zero, and the rest of this
section is how a divisor gets that property without a check on its value.

### The divisor is a type, and its zero is refused before the program runs

`Divisor<F>` is a slot of `F` that is not the zero member, and it has two constructors. Neither
inspects a value at run time: `ruling::never_a_runtime_check_and_one_lowered_path` rules out a test on
the divisor, and a constructor that classifies a slot and answers with a maybe is the same test moved
one call earlier.

`Divisor::<F>::constant::<B>()` takes the slot as a const parameter. Its obligation refuses a `B`
outside the slot range, and a `B` at which `phase + B` is zero, so a constant zero divisor stops the
build where the constant is named.

`Divisor::<F>::of(slot)` takes a slot read at run time, and is admitted exactly where no slot at all
names the zero. With the phase `n / d` in lowest terms, `phase + b` is zero only at `b = -n / d`, an
integer only when `d` is one. So where `d` is past one no slot is the zero, member or not, and a
half-offset grid qualifies. Where `d` is one the slot `-n` exists, and a slot is not typed as a member
of its format, so `of` would let it through whether or not the range holds it. It is refused there.

So every `Divisor` names a non-zero factor by construction: `constant` refuses the zero and every slot
outside the range, and `of` exists only where no slot is the zero. The step reads the factor and never
asks whether it is zero. That is the compromise of this stance, stated rather than hidden: a whole-phase
format divides only by constants, a plain integer range and a consumer's range that leaves the zero
out alike. Admitting the second needs a slot typed by membership, under the ticket
`arvo_a_runtime_divisor_over_a_whole_phase_range_without_the`. `Divisor::slot` reads the slot back.

### The exact step

Write `u = r^max(e, 0)` and `v = r^max(-e, 0)`. The position is the numerator
`d (n + a d) v - n (n + b d) u` over the denominator `d (n + b d) u`, both negated when `n + b d` is
below zero so that `D` is positive. Unlike the product's, this `D` depends on the divisor, so it is
formed per call in the four limbs, which the obligation guarantees for every member divisor.
`d (n + a d) v` is under `2^190` times `v`, the phase's term `n (n + b d) u` is at most `2^63` times
`D`, and a numerator that leaves the limbs is a step not formed. Then the shared ending places it.

### What it refuses, and what is not yet admitted

One obligation carries three conditions, forced by `quotient_position` and asked without forcing by
`is_divisible`, both reading one array of the three verdicts, so the two cannot disagree. Only the first
is a refusal by design.

A phase whose denominator is zero names no member, and nothing is computed for it.

A magnitude-indexed quantum is not yet admitted, for the reason the product gives: the applied map has
no magnitude coordinate to land the quotient at. The ticket is
`arvo_quotient_and_conversion_over_a_magnitude_indexed_quantu`.

A step not formed in the four limbs at some member pair is not yet admitted, under the remainder
coordinate's ticket `arvo_s_exact_position_carries_no_remainder_denominator_past`: a denominator
`d |n + b d| u` past `2^256 - 1` at some member divisor, or a numerator past it at some pair.
`|n + b d|` is affine in `b`, so the two range ends decide the denominator, and each of the
numerator's two terms is affine in one slot, so its magnitude over the box of slots is greatest at one
of the four corners the range ends make. The divisors next to the pole do not enter, since the
numerator divides by nothing.

A formed step has a position, as the shared ending says, so nothing about the slot index is asked. At a
negative exponent the quotient by a divisor next to the pole grows by `r^-e`, so a narrow range at a
fine quantum names positions far past the index; they are admitted, and the map reads their floor. So
`MIN / -1` over the signed 64-bit range at `e = -64` is the position `2^127`, one past the index's top,
and wraps to slot 0.

